WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.520
Did you ever watch an anime and think to yourself:
‘I wish I could watch that without having

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:07.360
to read the subtitles all the time.’

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:11.840
Well, I’ve definitely been there and it
was one of the reasons that got me into learning

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:13.840
Japanese in the first place.

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:18.000
And if you’re curious how I managed to get
to exactly that point today, in this video

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:22.000
I will be sharing with you guys how I got
started with Japanese, the struggles I had

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:27.200
with it and 3 big tips I have for everyone
interested in starting Japanese themselves

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:29.840
so avoid the same struggles I had.

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:43.200
As a quick disclaimer before we start, this
is not gonna be a video about how to become

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:48.800
fluent in Japanese in 3 weeks or something,
because the sad truth is, that’s not how

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:50.240
language learning works.

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:53.440
Learning Japanese will take time and effort.

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:59.760
So, what I’m gonna share with you, are some
valuable insights I learned in almost 4 years

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:04.800
of Japanese study, that will hopefully make
your learning-experience a lot more effective

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:05.760
and fun.

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:08.240
First of all: Is it worth it?

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:13.760
Being able to watch Anime without subtitles
has paid off in a number of ways for me.

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:19.760
I can focus on the plot and the artwork instead
of reading the subs, that are often very liberal

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:21.920
and sometimes straight-out bad.

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:27.040
Through Japanese I can understand the dialogue
and cultural context of the story way better.

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:32.400
But most importantly learning Japanese has
pushed me to move to Japan, broaden my world

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:35.680
view, see more places and meet new people.

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:38.800
So yes, it’s 100% worth it.

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:42.480
The 3 tips that I want to share with you based
on journey into the language of Anime and

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:46.400
Manga are Passion, Time and Strategy.

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:48.240
3 things you have to do.

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:51.280
Or as I like to call it PTSD.

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:54.080
Let’s look at each of these in detail.

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:58.800
The first thing you need before you even start
studying Japanese, is passion.

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:04.160
You need to have some sort of passion for
Japanese if you want to study it.

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:08.400
I’m gonna assume you are interested in Anime
if you are watching this video, so that’s

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:10.560
definitely a very solid start in my book.

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:17.040
Since I’ve always been a big fan of fantasy
and science fiction I was always pretty interested

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:21.360
in Japanese history and culture through a
number of books and movies.

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:26.560
Add to that my general love for Japanese food
and my discovery of anime and manga at the

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:31.120
end of high school, I was pretty much set
up for giving Japanese a shot.

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:36.640
I started roughly 3 and a half years ago,
by taking a couple of very basic and very

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:39.280
free Japanese courses at my university (Thank
you European education).

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:44.480
These courses took place maybe 3 times a month,
but at the time, I already felt like I was

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:45.920
learning a lot.

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:52.000
And so, when I managed to get an internship
in Tokyo back in 2018, I went to Japan thinking

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:56.320
that I already had a pretty good grasp of
the Japanese language over all.

00:02:56.320 --> 00:03:02.480
It took me exactly 5 minutes after arriving
at Haneda airport in Tokyo to find out that

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:07.200
I knew exactly nothing, a phenomenon that
we’ll be discussing I more detail later.

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:13.120
However, I never lost my passion for the language
and there was always stuff that I wanted to

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:15.680
either watch or read in Japanese.

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:22.720
The reason this is so important is that you
have to consume a lot of content in Japanese

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:27.200
and so you will want to find some you actually
love so much regardless of the language that

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:32.880
listening to it or reading it will be fun,
because it’s something that you love always.

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:37.920
Whether that is watching anime or listening
to a podcast about the herbs and fungi of

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:40.160
Hokkaido doesn’t really matter.

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:44.791
All that matters is that you enjoy it and
you can consume tons of it (insert image of

00:03:44.791 --> 00:03:45.280
mushrooms).

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:49.440
The second really important thing you should
be aware of is time.

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:56.400
Learning any language is a long-term project
and Japanese is one of the hardest languages

00:03:56.400 --> 00:04:02.000
to learn if you’re an English native speaker
or pretty much any other Indo-germanic language.

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:06.000
I know that there are tons of books and videos
out there that say that you can learn any

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:12.880
language in x months or even weeks, but the
truth is languages take time and work.

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:18.480
After the initial blow to my confidence at
Haneda airport, I did something that changed

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:20.960
my learning process significantly.

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:25.200
I started studying a little bit of Japanese
every, single, day.

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:30.640
I started by mostly working with some textbooks
I still had from my college course, grind

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:36.880
out tons of flashcards and forced myself to
learn the classic “1000 Kanji within a year”

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:37.380
approach.

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:43.120
The consistency strongly increased my learning
speed and also allowed me to keep newly-learned

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:46.880
words and concepts way easier in my long-term
memory.

00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:51.440
As mentioned before, you want to consume as
much of your target language as you can and

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:55.680
immerse yourself in it and that is something
that again takes a while.

00:04:55.680 --> 00:05:00.720
So being aware of that fact and being patient
and determined to pull through is one of the

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:03.680
most important premises for getting to your
desired level.

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:09.200
No matter if that’s watching anime without
subtitles or writing an academic paper, you

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:13.280
have to be aware that this is a long-term
project you’re starting.

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:15.680
It’s not happening overnight, folks.

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:20.800
The main thing here is that new things have
a really high learning curve at the start,

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:23.040
because, yeah, everything is new.

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:26.800
So it really feels like you’re getting really
good really fast.

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:31.600
But then it suddenly gets harder and harder
to learn the same amount of new things and

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:34.400
there will be the first major slump in your
learning.

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:39.120
This has happened a bunch of times for me
with different things, like languages or playing

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:39.840
a new instrument.

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:46.000
However slow you think your pace is, what
matters is you push through this dip.

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:48.480
It’s where most people quit.

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:51.600
Called the Dunning-Kruger effect.

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:57.040
It’s a cognitive bias in which people with
low ability at a task tend to overestimate

00:05:57.040 --> 00:06:02.000
their ability and people with a higher ability
tend to underestimate this ability.

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:05.840
It’s one of the most eye-opening concepts
I have ever come across.

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:11.040
Let’s take a graph and mark my true level
of competence, let’s say in Japanese, on

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:18.160
the x axis and my perceived level of competence
on the y axis, in other words how good am

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:21.200
I vs how good do I think I am.

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:23.360
Due to that very steep learning curve at the&nbsp;

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:26.240
start, most people tend to&nbsp;
drastically overestimate

00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:30.480
their true skill level that is nicely called
the mountain of stupid.

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:35.840
Once you actually hit that point, just as
I did when I arrived in Japan, you suddenly

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:40.480
realise that this was actually an illusion
and you fall into the so-called valley of

00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:43.280
despair, where you feel like you know nothing.

00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:49.200
This is the point most people quit and it
is this point that you will have to grind

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:49.700
through.

00:06:50.320 --> 00:06:56.000
After some time you will get better at judging
your true level better and better and motivation

00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:59.200
will rise as you actually get a better hold
of the language.

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:04.320
And so going into my second year of serious
Japanese study, I decided there was no way

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:10.720
around it, I would have to start actually
consuming Japanese content - like a lot of

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:11.680
content.

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:18.560
So I sat down and at a painfully slow speed
started to grind my way through my first Japanese

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:20.240
book - Harry Potter.

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:25.440
I’m gonna be honest with you, it felt pretty
much impossible for a long time.

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:32.160
I had to constantly look up words, figure
out grammar patterns, read one sentence over

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:37.600
and over for at leat 30 minutes because I
couldn’t understand it - you get the point.

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:38.960
So to sum it up.

00:07:38.960 --> 00:07:41.440
Be aware that Japanese will take time.

00:07:42.080 --> 00:07:47.200
Be patient, because I guarantee you that even
when it feels like you know nothing, you’re

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:48.560
actually improving.

00:07:48.560 --> 00:07:55.200
If you’re like me and need something more
precise to work with, go make 30 small boxes,

00:07:55.200 --> 00:08:01.280
mark the date of the next 30 days on there
and then cross off every day with a red pen

00:08:01.280 --> 00:08:07.120
whenever you studied some Japanese regardless
if it’s only for 5 minutes or for an hour.

00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:09.520
This is actually one of my favourite ways
of habit building.

00:08:13.920 --> 00:08:18.880
The third tip I have for you (and I believe
is the most important) based on my own experience

00:08:18.880 --> 00:08:24.240
is that you can save yourself a lot of time
and pointless grinding when you have a proper

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:26.400
strategy when it comes to studying.

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:31.360
While becoming more consistent with my studies
clearly improved my learning speed, the way

00:08:31.360 --> 00:08:37.200
I was approaching things in the end left me
with a ton of individual words and Kanji and

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:38.960
no way of putting them together.

00:08:38.960 --> 00:08:42.480
You don’t want to do it like me and study
all over the place.

00:08:43.120 --> 00:08:45.520
The first thing you want is a goal.

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:51.200
This sounds trivial, but most people actually
don’t know their goal for studying Japanese.

00:08:51.200 --> 00:08:55.760
But I found that having a definite goal makes
a big difference in Japanese study.

00:08:55.760 --> 00:09:02.320
Whether you want to be able to order food,
watch anime without subtitles or do research

00:09:02.320 --> 00:09:08.640
in biochemistry, there are definitely different
skills required and ultimately, a different

00:09:08.640 --> 00:09:11.120
way of approaching Japanese language learning.

00:09:11.120 --> 00:09:15.840
So being aware of where you actually wanna
go is essential.

00:09:17.440 --> 00:09:22.000
I didn’t really have one when I started,
but once I realised that I needed a goal,

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:25.840
I decided that I wanted to be able to make
videos in Japanese at some point.

00:09:26.480 --> 00:09:31.360
My suggestion to you is to sit down, think
about this, and then go write it down so you

00:09:31.360 --> 00:09:32.320
don’t forget.

00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:34.640
That way it will be easier to track your progress.

00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:42.000
Then, I personally find it very important
to be aware of your own strengths and weaknesses.

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:50.320
For instance, some things I would say I’m
pretty good at studying are large vocabulary,

00:09:50.320 --> 00:09:57.120
my pronunciation and pitch accent as well
as the ability to read and listen to Japanese

00:09:57.120 --> 00:10:02.480
content based on context and without necessarily
knowing every single word in a sentence.

00:10:02.480 --> 00:10:08.800
My biggest weaknesses are writing, Keigo,
which is formal Japanese communication, and

00:10:08.800 --> 00:10:09.520
my grammar.

00:10:13.680 --> 00:10:18.240
Being aware of what you’re good at and what
you’re bad at will help you figuring out

00:10:18.240 --> 00:10:19.520
what and how to study.

00:10:19.520 --> 00:10:19.760
For example my reading was terrible, so I
read more.

00:10:19.760 --> 00:10:23.040
However, take note that you will always find
skills you’re poor at.

00:10:24.080 --> 00:10:29.440
For me, after starting to read more Japanese
content, I realised my listening was lagging

00:10:29.440 --> 00:10:32.320
behind, so I listened more.

00:10:32.320 --> 00:10:34.720
then now it’s my writing, and so on.

00:10:35.920 --> 00:10:41.280
I personally think it’s completely okay
to study something you find interesting, it’s

00:10:41.280 --> 00:10:46.960
actually in accordance to tip number 1, passion,
however always make sure that it’s structured

00:10:46.960 --> 00:10:50.160
and that you are aware of what you’re working
on at the moment.

00:10:50.160 --> 00:10:55.040
For instance, if you are really into watching
anime, be aware that’s gonna be listening

00:10:55.040 --> 00:10:55.840
practice.

00:10:55.840 --> 00:10:59.840
If you’re really into learning new kanji,
well that’s gonna be kanji practice.

00:11:00.560 --> 00:11:05.280
And if you’re really into reading novels,
then that’s gonna be reading practice.

00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:07.520
Just be mindful of what you’re doing.

00:11:08.320 --> 00:11:14.240
Implementing these things, very slowly, I
got better, and better and better and finally

00:11:14.240 --> 00:11:17.840
I was able to read my books and manga pretty
comfortably.

00:11:17.840 --> 00:11:20.400
Not gonna lie that felt pretty good.

00:11:20.960 --> 00:11:27.040
Only now I had to address a new problem, my
reading was improving, but my listening skills

00:11:27.040 --> 00:11:28.640
were absolutely terrible.

00:11:29.200 --> 00:11:36.400
So I did the same thing again, this time with
Japanese podcasts and of course: Anime.

00:11:36.400 --> 00:11:40.880
Today, I have finally managed to find a way
more balanced way of doing things, but I will

00:11:40.880 --> 00:11:46.720
admit that right now my writing is still lagging
far behind reading, listening and speaking.

00:11:47.520 --> 00:11:53.200
I’m still experimenting with new and different
leaning methods, but I now have a very solid

00:11:53.200 --> 00:11:55.840
base I can build up on.

00:11:57.120 --> 00:12:02.160
I think that these 3 tips can help you in
overcoming some of the common struggles that

00:12:02.160 --> 00:12:04.560
people experience when it comes to language
learning.

00:12:05.440 --> 00:12:08.320
Also makes your studies more effective and
fun.

00:12:08.320 --> 00:12:13.360
If you’re feeling that you’re not making
any progress, be aware that this takes time

00:12:13.360 --> 00:12:16.720
and that progress usually comes in break-through
episodes.

00:12:17.600 --> 00:12:21.760
If you ever feel like you are forgetting stuff,
this is normal.

00:12:21.760 --> 00:12:24.880
The important and more commonly used stuff
will stick.

00:12:25.600 --> 00:12:27.760
Those will always come back later.

00:12:27.760 --> 00:12:34.800
The same goes for feeling that you get 2 new
unknown things for every thing you learn.

00:12:36.640 --> 00:12:39.520
Remember the Dunning-Kruger effect and the
graph.

00:12:39.520 --> 00:12:41.760
It will become better over time.

00:12:41.760 --> 00:12:45.600
Plus, language learning never really stops,
literally.

00:12:46.400 --> 00:12:50.960
I’m feeling pretty damn fluent in English
at this point and even then I still learn

00:12:50.960 --> 00:12:53.040
new stuff all the time.

00:12:53.040 --> 00:12:58.400
If you have other things in your life threatening
to overshadow your Japanese studies, make

00:12:58.400 --> 00:13:04.160
sure to prioritise and make a habit out of
it, that you simply do every day.

00:13:04.160 --> 00:13:09.520
And to counter burn-out, make sure you do
things you enjoy and are passionate about.

00:13:09.520 --> 00:13:12.560
In the end this should be about having fun.

00:13:14.000 --> 00:13:18.720
If you can’t stand doing grammar today,
better watch some anime rather than skip your

00:13:18.720 --> 00:13:20.160
practice completely.

00:13:20.160 --> 00:13:22.320
Consistency over quantity.

00:13:22.320 --> 00:13:28.240
And so, if you can stick to these 3 things,
passion, time, strategy, and DO IT.

00:13:29.680 --> 00:13:34.080
I am sure that eventually, you will be able
to sit down and watch your favourite anime

00:13:34.080 --> 00:13:36.400
as well - without subtitles.

00:13:36.400 --> 00:13:36.720
;)

00:13:36.720 --> 00:13:38.720
I hope you enjoyed this video.

00:13:38.720 --> 00:13:42.880
If there is anything specific you want to
learn more about in another video, let me

00:13:42.880 --> 00:13:45.920
know in the comments below and don’t forget
to subscribe to

00:13:45.920 --> 00:13:46.960
the channel.

00:13:46.960 --> 00:13:48.400
Thanks guys, peace.

